          "title": "1.1 Layers of the Earth: Crust, Mantle, Core",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/13158590/pexels-photo-13158590.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hi2oamo4DGY",
          "content": "The Earth, a nearly spherical planet, is not a uniform solid body but is composed of several distinct concentric layers. These layers differ in terms of their chemical composition, temperature, pressure, and physical state (solid, liquid, semi-molten). Our understanding of these internal layers is largely derived from indirect evidence, primarily the study of seismic waves (P-waves and S-waves) generated by earthquakes. The speed, direction, and reflection/refraction of these waves as they travel through the Earth reveal changes in density and state of matter, allowing scientists to map the interior.\n\nThe Main Layers (from outermost to innermost):\n\nCrust:\n\nDescription: The Earth's outermost, thinnest, and coolest solid shell. It is brittle and can fracture, leading to faults and earthquakes. It is the layer upon which all life exists and all geological features familiar to us are found.\n\nComposition and Types:\n\nContinental Crust: Forms the landmasses.\n\nThickness: Averages 35-40 km, but can be up to 70-100 km thick under major mountain ranges (e.g., Himalayas, Andes).\n\nComposition: Primarily composed of less dense, granitic rocks (rich in silica and alumina – sometimes referred to as SiAl). Common rocks include granite, gneiss, and schist. Its overall composition is often generalized as granodiorite.\n\nDensity: Average density is about 2.7 g/cm³.\n\nAge: Can be very old, with some sections dating back nearly 4 billion years.\n\nOceanic Crust: Forms the seafloor.\n\nThickness: Thinner, averaging 5-10 km.\n\nComposition: Primarily composed of denser, basaltic rocks (rich in silica and magnesia – sometimes referred to as SiMa). Common rocks include basalt and gabbro.\n\nDensity: Average density is about 3.0 g/cm³.\n\nAge: Relatively young compared to continental crust, continuously being formed at mid-ocean ridges and destroyed at subduction zones. Oldest oceanic crust is about 180-200 million years old.\n\nMohorovičić Discontinuity (Moho): The boundary separating the crust from the underlying mantle. It is identified by a sudden increase in the velocity of seismic waves (especially P-waves), indicating a change in density and composition.\n\nMantle:\n\nDescription: Located beneath the crust, this is the thickest layer of the Earth, extending to a depth of about 2,900 km. It makes up about 84% of Earth's volume and about 67% of its mass.\n\nComposition: Composed mainly of dense silicate rocks rich in iron (Fe) and magnesium (Mg) (e.g., peridotite, containing minerals like olivine and pyroxene).\n\nPhysical State and Dynamics: The mantle is mostly solid rock, but on geological timescales (millions of years), it behaves as a very viscous, deformable fluid (exhibiting plasticity) due to extremely high temperatures and pressures. This allows for slow convection currents within the mantle, where hotter, less dense material rises, and cooler, denser material sinks. These convection currents are a primary driving force behind plate tectonics.\n\nSub-layers:\n\nUpper Mantle: Extends from the Moho down to about 660 km.\n\nLithosphere (Rigid Upper Mantle): The uppermost part of the mantle, extending from the Moho down to variable depths (typically 70-150 km, but can be thicker under old continental shields). It is cool, rigid, and brittle. The crust and this rigid upper portion of the mantle together form the lithosphere. The lithosphere is what is broken into the tectonic plates.\n\nAsthenosphere (Plastic Mantle): A mechanically weak, partially molten (about 1-5% molten rock, though some argue it's more about reduced rigidity due to temperature/pressure near melting point), and ductile layer within the upper mantle, located just below the lithosphere (typically extending from the base of the lithosphere to about 400 km depth, though boundaries can be gradational and variable, sometimes extending to 700 km). \"Astheno\" means weak. It is hotter and less rigid than the lithosphere, allowing it to flow slowly. The lithospheric plates \"float\" and move over this more easily flowing asthenosphere. The primary convection cells thought to drive plate motion occur within or involve the asthenosphere and deeper mantle.\n\nTransition Zone: Located between approximately 410 km and 660 km depth within the upper mantle. Seismic wave velocities increase in steps here due to phase changes in minerals (crystal structures transform to denser forms under increasing pressure).\n\nLower Mantle (sometimes called Mesosphere geophysically): Extends from about 660 km down to the core-mantle boundary (around 2900 km). Despite very high temperatures, the immense pressure keeps this layer largely solid and more rigid (stronger) than the asthenosphere, though very slow convection likely extends through it.\n\nGutenberg Discontinuity (Core-Mantle Boundary - CMB): The boundary between the lower mantle and the outer core, at a depth of about 2900 km. Marked by a sharp decrease in P-wave velocity and the stopping of S-waves (indicating the outer core is liquid).\n\nCore:\n\nDescription: The Earth's innermost, central region, extremely hot and dense. It accounts for about 15% of Earth's volume but about 32% of its mass due to its high density.\n\nComposition: Composed primarily of an iron-nickel (Fe-Ni) alloy, with smaller amounts of other elements like sulfur, oxygen, or silicon thought to be present.\n\nDivisions:\n\nOuter Core:\n\nPhysical State: A liquid layer, about 2,200 km thick, extending from about 2900 km to 5150 km depth.\n\nTemperature: Very high, estimated between 4400°C (at top) and 6100°C (at bottom).\n\nKey Role: The movement (convection currents) of the electrically conductive molten iron within the outer core, likely influenced by Earth's rotation (Coriolis effect), is believed to generate the Earth's magnetic field through a self-sustaining dynamo process (geodynamo theory). The magnetic field extends into space (magnetosphere) and protects Earth from harmful solar wind particles.\n\nInner Core:\n\nPhysical State: A solid sphere, with a radius of about 1,220 km (about 70% of the Moon's radius).\n\nTemperature: Extremely hot, estimated to be between 5200°C and perhaps as high as 7000°C (comparable to the surface of the Sun).\n\nPressure: Immense pressure (over 3 million atmospheres) prevents the iron-nickel alloy from melting, despite the extremely high temperatures; it is effectively \"frozen\" by pressure.\n\nDensity: Very high, around 12.6 to 13.0 g/cm³.\n\nRotation: Some evidence suggests the inner core may rotate slightly faster than the rest of the planet, and its structure may be anisotropic (having different properties in different directions).\n\n(Suggested further detail: Add a simple diagram showing P-wave and S-wave paths through the Earth, illustrating S-wave shadow zone due to liquid outer core, and P-wave shadow zone/refraction due to changes at core boundaries. This visually explains how we know the layers' states.)",
