          "title": "2.2 Weather Elements: Temperature, Pressure, Wind, Humidity, Precipitation",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/19545983/pexels-photo-19545983.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A1uzCvarF5g",
          "content": "Weather Elements: The specific atmospheric variables whose values at a given time and place define the state of the weather.\n\nTemperature (Air Temperature):\n\nMeasurement: Thermometers (liquid-in-glass, bimetallic, digital/thermistor). Housed in a Stevenson Screen (louvered white box) to shield from direct solar radiation and allow free air circulation, for standardized readings usually 1.25-2m above ground.\n\nDiurnal (Daily) Temperature Variation: Typically, lowest just before sunrise, highest in early to mid-afternoon (lag after solar noon due to time for ground to heat up and transfer to air).\n\nAnnual Temperature Variation: Follows seasons.\n\nIsotherms: Lines on a weather map connecting points of equal temperature.\n\nTemperature Range: Difference between maximum and minimum temperature over a period (daily, monthly, annual).\n\nAtmospheric Pressure (Air Pressure):\n\nMeasurement: Barometers. Mercury barometer (Torricelli) balances weight of air column against mercury column. Aneroid barometer uses a flexible, sealed metal chamber. Barograph records pressure continuously.\n\nVariation with Altitude: Pressure decreases rapidly with increasing altitude (less air above).\n\nHorizontal Variation: Drives wind.\n\nLow Pressure Systems (Lows, Depressions, Cyclones): Areas of lower pressure than surroundings. Characterized by converging air at surface, rising air, often leading to cloud formation and precipitation. Associated with unsettled weather. In Northern Hemisphere, winds blow counter-clockwise into a low.\n\nHigh Pressure Systems (Highs, Anticyclones): Areas of higher pressure than surroundings. Characterized by diverging air at surface, sinking air (subsidence), often leading to clear skies and stable, fair weather. In Northern Hemisphere, winds blow clockwise out of a high.\n\nIsobars: Lines on weather maps connecting points of equal sea-level pressure. Spacing of isobars indicates pressure gradient (closer lines = steeper gradient = stronger winds).\n\nWind:\n\nMeasurement:\n\nSpeed: Anemometer (cup anemometer common). Units: m/s, km/h, knots.\n\nDirection: Wind vane (points into the wind, indicating direction from which wind blows, e.g., North wind blows from N to S). Reported using cardinal directions (N, E, S, W) or degrees (0°=N, 90°=E, 180°=S, 270°=W).\n\nCoriolis Effect: Deflection of moving air (and water) due to Earth's rotation. To the right in Northern Hemisphere, to the left in Southern Hemisphere. Increases with latitude and wind speed. Affects large-scale wind patterns (e.g., formation of cyclones, trade winds).\n\nFriction: Surface friction (from terrain, buildings, vegetation) slows wind near surface and alters its direction.\n\nLocal Winds: Caused by local variations in temperature and pressure (e.g., land and sea breezes, mountain and valley breezes).\n\nHumidity:\n\nMeasurement: Hygrometers. Sling psychrometer has a dry-bulb thermometer (measures air T) and a wet-bulb thermometer (bulb covered with wet wick; evaporation causes cooling). Difference between dry-bulb and wet-bulb temperatures (wet-bulb depression) is used with tables to find relative humidity and dew point. Hair hygrometers use change in length of hair with humidity. Electronic sensors are common now.\n\nRelationship with Temperature: Warm air can hold more water vapor than cold air. If temperature drops (e.g., at night), relative humidity increases even if absolute water vapor content stays same. If cooled to dew point, saturation occurs, and condensation (dew, fog, cloud) may form.\n\nPrecipitation:\n\nFormation (Mechanisms of Uplift Causing Cooling and Condensation):\n\nConvectional Rainfall: Sun heats ground, warm moist air rises, cools adiabatically (by expansion), condenses to form cumulus/cumulonimbus clouds, leading to showers (often intense, localized, common in tropics and summer mid-latitudes). Visible as \"rain towers\".\n\nOrographic (Relief) Rainfall: Moist air is forced to rise over a mountain barrier, cools adiabatically, condenses, forms clouds and precipitation on windward side. Leeward side is often in a rain shadow (drier).\n\nFrontal (Cyclonic) Rainfall: Occurs at weather fronts where warm, moist air mass meets and is forced to rise over a colder, denser air mass. Common in mid-latitudes with depressions (low-pressure systems). (Warm front - gentle, prolonged rain; Cold front - often heavier, shorter showers, thunderstorms).\n\nMeasurement: Rain gauge (cylindrical container, often with funnel, measures depth of rain in mm or inches). Automated tipping-bucket rain gauges.\n\nIsohyets: Lines on a map connecting points of equal rainfall over a period.\n\n(Suggested further detail: Diagrams for each uplift mechanism causing precipitation (convectional, orographic with rain shadow, frontal – warm and cold). Diagrams showing how pressure gradient, Coriolis, and friction influence surface wind direction relative to isobars. A more detailed look at the Beaufort scale or common cloud classification by form and altitude.)",
