          "content": "Rivers perform three main types of geological work:\nErosion (Fluvial Erosion): The wearing away and removal of rock and soil from the river channel (bed and banks).\nMechanisms:\nHydraulic Action: The force of flowing water dislodging material. Air in cracks is compressed and expands, weakening rock.\nAbrasion (Corrasion): Sediment (load) carried by the river grinds against the bed and banks, like sandpaper.\nAttrition: Rock particles in the load collide with each other, becoming smaller and more rounded.\nSolution (Corrosion): Dissolving of soluble rocks (e.g., limestone) by river water.\nTransportation: The movement of the river's load (eroded material).\nMechanisms:\nTraction: Large boulders rolled along the bed.\nSaltation: Smaller pebbles bounced along the bed.\nSuspension: Fine particles (silt, clay) carried within the water flow.\nSolution: Dissolved minerals carried in the water.\nDeposition: The dropping of sediment by the river when its velocity and energy decrease.\nOccurs when gradient lessens, discharge decreases, or river enters a standing body of water. Heavier particles are dropped first.\n(Further Detail: Mention factors affecting rate of erosion e.g., velocity, load, bedrock type. Note that velocity is key for both transportation capacity and deposition.)",
