          "content": "River processes create distinct landforms along the river's course, often divided into upper, middle, and lower stages.\nUpper Course (Youthful stage, steep gradient):\nDominant process: Vertical erosion.\nLandforms: V-shaped valleys (deep, narrow), interlocking spurs, waterfalls, rapids, gorges, potholes.\nMiddle Course (Mature stage, gentler gradient):\nDominant processes: Lateral erosion and transportation become more significant.\nLandforms: Wider valleys, meanders (bends in the river – erosion on outer bank/river cliff, deposition on inner bank/slip-off slope), beginnings of a floodplain.\nLower Course (Old age stage, very gentle gradient):\nDominant process: Deposition.\nLandforms: Wide, flat floodplains (built by deposition of alluvium during floods), levees (natural embankments), oxbow lakes (cut-off meander loops), braided channels (if high sediment load), deltas (fan-shaped deposits at river mouth where it enters a lake/sea - e.g., arcuate, bird's foot).\n(Further Detail: For each landform, briefly explain its formation. E.g., waterfall formation due to differential erosion of hard and soft rock layers.)",
