          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wxo6wPOyUnI",
          "content": "Coastlines are shaped by the interaction of marine and terrestrial forces.\nWaves:\nGenerated by wind transferring energy to water.\nIn shallow water approaching shore: Wave velocity decreases, wavelength shortens, height increases, and wave breaks, releasing energy.\nSwash: Water rushing up the beach.\nBackwash: Water returning down the beach.\nCoastal Erosion (by waves):\nHydraulic Action: Force of waves compresses air in cracks.\nAbrasion: Waves hurl sediment against cliffs.\nAttrition: Sediment particles collide and break down.\nSolution: Dissolving of susceptible rocks by seawater.\nTides:\nRegular rise and fall of sea level due to gravitational pull of Moon and Sun.\nSpring Tides: Large tidal range (Sun, Earth, Moon aligned - New/Full Moon).\nNeap Tides: Small tidal range (Sun and Moon at right angles to Earth - Quarter Moons).\nTidal currents can erode and transport sediment in coastal areas.\nLongshore Drift:\nMovement of sediment (sand, shingle) along the coastline.\nCaused by waves approaching the shore at an oblique angle. Swash carries sediment up the beach at an angle; backwash carries it straight down. Result is a net zigzag movement of sediment along the shore. Important for building depositional landforms.\n(Further Detail: Briefly distinguish constructive vs. destructive waves by their relative swash/backwash strength.)",
