          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sKJoXdrOT70",
          "content": "Ecosystem: A community of living organisms (biotic components) interacting with each other and with their non-living physical and chemical environment (abiotic components), linked together by flows of energy and cycling of nutrients.\nEcosystems can vary greatly in size (from a small pond to a vast forest or ocean).\nAll ecosystems require an energy source (usually the sun).\nComponents of an Ecosystem:\nAbiotic Components (Non-living):\nSolar energy (sunlight).\nClimate (temperature, precipitation, wind, humidity).\nWater (availability, quality, salinity).\nSoil or Substrate (minerals, nutrients, pH, texture).\nAtmosphere (gases like O₂, CO₂, N₂).\nTopography (slope, aspect, altitude).\nDisturbances (fire, floods, storms).\nBiotic Components (Living Organisms): Grouped by their role in energy flow (trophic levels):\nProducers (Autotrophs): Organisms that make their own organic food from inorganic sources, primarily through photosynthesis. They form the base of food webs.\nExamples: Plants, algae, some bacteria (cyanobacteria).\nConsumers (Heterotrophs): Organisms that obtain energy by feeding on other organisms.\nPrimary Consumers (Herbivores): Eat producers (e.g., deer, grasshoppers, zooplankton).\nSecondary Consumers (Carnivores/Omnivores): Eat primary consumers (e.g., foxes, fish that eat zooplankton).\nTertiary/Quaternary Consumers: Eat other consumers.\nOmnivores: Eat both plants and animals.\nDecomposers (Detritivores): Organisms that break down dead organic matter (detritus – dead plants, animals, waste) and release nutrients back into the ecosystem for reuse by producers. Crucial for nutrient cycling.\nExamples: Bacteria, fungi, earthworms, some insects.\nEnergy Flow and Nutrient Cycling: Two fundamental processes in ecosystems.\nEnergy flows in one direction (sun → producers → consumers → decomposers, with loss at each step).\nNutrients are cycled between biotic and abiotic components.\n(Further Detail: Diagram showing abiotic and biotic components interacting, with arrows illustrating energy flow and nutrient cycling conceptually.)",
