          "title": "7.2 Agriculture: Types of Farming, Land Use, Commercial vs. Subsistence",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/757239/pexels-photo-757239.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=idBmaUTemb4",
          "content": "(Builds on Ag. Sci. Ch 1.3, now with more explicit geographic focus on land use and global patterns).\nAgricultural Geography: Studies the spatial distribution of agricultural activities, the factors influencing them, and their impact on the landscape and environment.\n\nTypes of Farming Systems (Classification often based on inputs, outputs, scale, purpose):\n\nSubsistence Farming:\n\nProduction is primarily for the consumption of the farmer and their family. Little or no surplus for sale.\n\nLow inputs (labor-intensive, simple tools, limited capital/chemicals).\n\nOften small landholdings (fragmented).\n\nHigh diversification (polyculture) to meet varied needs and reduce risk.\n\nYields per unit area often low.\n\nCommon in many LDCs.\n\nExamples: Shifting cultivation (slash-and-burn), nomadic herding, intensive subsistence agriculture (e.g., wet rice cultivation in parts of Asia).\n\nCommercial Farming:\n\nProduction is primarily for sale in the market to generate profit.\n\nHigh inputs (capital-intensive, mechanization, improved seeds, fertilizers, pesticides).\n\nOften large landholdings (can also be intensive on smaller specialized farms).\n\nSpecialization (monoculture or few high-value products).\n\nHigh yields per unit area or per unit labor.\n\nMarket-oriented. Common in MDCs and parts of LDCs.\n\nExamples: Plantation agriculture (tea, coffee, rubber, bananas), large-scale grain farming, commercial livestock ranching, dairy farming, market gardening (truck farming).\n\nMixed Farming: (Combines crops and livestock on same farm - can be subsistence or commercial).\n\nExtensive Farming vs. Intensive Farming:\n\nExtensive: Large areas of land, low inputs per unit area (e.g., cattle ranching on rangelands).\n\nIntensive: Small areas of land, high inputs per unit area to maximize yield (e.g., market gardening, greenhouse cultivation, intensive rice paddies).\n\nAgricultural Land Use Patterns (Influenced by von Thünen's Model conceptually):\nJohann von Thünen (early 19th C) developed a model to explain agricultural land use patterns around a central market city, based on transport costs and land rent.\n\nCore Idea: Perishable and high-value crops with high transport costs are typically grown closer to the market. Less perishable, lower-value crops with lower transport costs (or extensive land use) are grown further away.\n\nConcentric Rings (Idealized Model):\n\nMarket gardening and dairy (highly perishable, high transport cost) closest to city.\n\nForestry (for fuel/building, heavy to transport) next.\n\nIntensive crop rotation.\n\nExtensive field crops, pasture.\n\nRanching/Livestock grazing (most extensive) furthest away.\n\nModifications: Model modified by factors like soil fertility, topography, faster transport, multiple markets, government policies. However, core concept of distance to market influencing land use still relevant.\n\nGlobal Patterns of Agriculture: Distribution of different farming systems is influenced by climate, soil, relief, culture, technology, economic factors, and government policies.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A world map showing general distribution of major agricultural systems (e.g., shifting cultivation, nomadic herding, intensive subsistence rice, commercial grain, plantation, ranching). A simplified diagram of von Thünen's model of agricultural land use.)",
