          "content": "The Service Sector (Tertiary and Quaternary):\nLargest sector in most developed economies and growing significantly in developing ones.\n1. Transport Services:\n\nMovement of people and goods. Essential for economic activity, trade, and social interaction.\n\nModes: Road (cars, buses, trucks), Rail, Air, Water (shipping, ferries), Pipelines (for oil, gas).\n\nInfrastructure: Roads, railways, ports, airports, canals.\n\nFactors influencing transport networks: Physical geography, economic development, technology, government investment.\n\nTransport costs are a key factor in location of economic activities and trade.\n\n2. Financial Services:\n\nBanking, insurance, investment services, stock markets, real estate finance.\n\nFacilitate capital flows, manage risk, enable investment and economic transactions.\n\nOften concentrated in major global financial centers (e.g., New York, London, Tokyo, Hong Kong, Singapore).\n\n3. Tourism:\n\nTravel for leisure, recreation, business, or other purposes. A major global service industry.\n\nTypes: Cultural tourism, ecotourism, beach tourism, adventure tourism, medical tourism, business tourism.\n\nFactors Influencing Tourism: Natural attractions (beaches, mountains, wildlife), cultural heritage, infrastructure (hotels, transport), safety and security, marketing, affordability.\n\nEconomic impacts (revenue, employment) and socio-cultural/environmental impacts (positive and negative).\n\n4. Other Services: Retail, wholesale, healthcare, education, government, IT services, entertainment.\n\nGlobalization and International Trade Patterns:\n\nGlobalization (Economic Aspect - Review from previous chapters): Increasing economic interdependence and integration of countries through cross-border flows of goods, services, capital, technology, and labor.\n\nDriven by:\n\nTechnological advances (transport, communication - especially internet).\n\nTrade liberalization (reduction of trade barriers like tariffs and quotas, e.g., through WTO, regional trade agreements).\n\nGrowth of Multinational Corporations (MNCs).\n\nMovement of capital and investment.\n\nInternational Trade: Exchange of goods and services between countries.\n\nReasons for Trade:\n\nDifferences in resource endowments (comparative advantage).\n\nEconomies of scale in production.\n\nIncreased product variety and consumer choice.\n\nAccess to goods not produced domestically.\n\nMajor Global Trade Patterns/Blocs:\n\nDominance of developed countries (though emerging economies like China, India, Brazil are increasingly important).\n\nGrowth of regional trade blocs (e.g., European Union - EU, United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement - USMCA, African Continental Free Trade Area - AfCFTA, ASEAN Free Trade Area - AFTA). These aim to reduce trade barriers among member countries.\n\nKey Trade Issues: Terms of trade, trade imbalances, protectionism vs. free trade, impact on developing countries, labor and environmental standards in trade.\n\nRole of Information Technology (IT) and E-commerce in Global Trade: Facilitates international business transactions, marketing, logistics.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Images representing different service sector activities (e.g., a busy port, a stock exchange, a tourist destination, an IT office). A world map showing major global trade flows or major regional trade blocs. A brief note on the World Trade Organization (WTO) and its role.)",
