          "title": "11.4 Economic Activities: Coffee, Tea, Tourism, Mining, Industrial Hubs",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/17687551/pexels-photo-17687551.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8AwP_vWsAng",
          "content": "**The economies of East and Southern African countries are diverse, with varying reliance on agriculture, resource extraction, tourism, and emerging industrial/service sectors.**\n\n**Agriculture:**\n* Still a major employer and contributor to GDP in many countries.\n* **Cash Crops for Export:**\n* **Coffee:** Major export for Ethiopia (origin of Arabica coffee), Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, Rwanda, Burundi. High-quality Arabica common.\n* **Tea:** Kenya (major global exporter), Malawi, Uganda, Zimbabwe, Tanzania. Grown in highland areas.\n* **Cotton:** Tanzania, Zimbabwe, Mozambique, Uganda.\n* **Tobacco:** Zimbabwe, Malawi, Tanzania, Mozambique.\n* **Sugar Cane:** South Africa, Swaziland, Mauritius, Kenya.\n* **Horticulture (Flowers, Fruits, Vegetables for Export):** Kenya, Ethiopia, South Africa, Zimbabwe are significant exporters to Europe and Middle East.\n* **Cashew nuts, sisal, pyrethrum** (natural insecticide).\n* **Subsistence Farming:** Production of staple food crops (maize, cassava, sorghum, millet, bananas, beans) for local consumption is widespread.\n* **Livestock:** Cattle, sheep, goats are important for meat, milk, wealth, and cultural reasons, especially in savanna and semi-arid areas (pastoralism and mixed farming).\n\n**Tourism:**\n* A major and growing industry in many East and Southern African countries, driven by:\n* **Wildlife Tourism (Safaris):** Iconic national parks and game reserves (e.g., Serengeti/Maasai Mara - Tanzania/Kenya; Kruger - South Africa; Okavango Delta - Botswana; Etosha - Namibia; South Luangwa - Zambia). \"Big Five\" (lion, leopard, elephant, rhino, buffalo) and other wildlife.\n* **Coastal Tourism:** Beaches and marine attractions (e.g., Zanzibar, Kenyan coast, Seychelles, Mauritius, Mozambique).\n* **Mountain Tourism:** (e.g., Kilimanjaro, Mount Kenya, Drakensberg).\n* **Cultural Tourism.**\n* Provides foreign exchange, employment, and incentives for conservation (if well-managed).\n* **Challenges:** Environmental impact, leakage of revenue, infrastructure needs, security.\n\n**Mining:**\n* Region is rich in mineral resources.\n* **Major Minerals and Producers:**\n* **Gold:** South Africa (historically world's largest), Tanzania, Ghana (West Africa, but significant in African context), Zimbabwe.\n* **Diamonds:** Botswana (major producer by value), South Africa, Namibia, Angola, DRC (Central Africa).\n* **Platinum Group Metals (PGMs):** South Africa (world's largest reserves and producer), Zimbabwe.\n* **Copper:** Zambia and DRC (Copperbelt).\n* **Coal:** South Africa (major producer and exporter).\n* **Chromite:** South Africa, Zimbabwe.\n* **Cobalt:** DRC, Zambia.\n* **Oil and Gas:** Angola, Mozambique (emerging natural gas), exploration in other areas.\n* Economic importance, but also potential for environmental damage and social issues (\"resource curse\" concerns if revenues not well managed).\n\n**Industry and Manufacturing:**\n* Generally less developed than in some other world regions, but growing in some countries.\n* **South Africa:** Most industrialized country in the region (diversified manufacturing, automotive, chemicals, food processing, metal products).\n* **Industrial Hubs/Growth Areas:** Gauteng (South Africa), Nairobi (Kenya), Addis Ababa (Ethiopia), Dar es Salaam (Tanzania).\n* **Focus:** Often agro-processing, textiles, consumer goods, assembly.\n* **Challenges:** Infrastructure (energy, transport), skilled labor, access to finance, competition.\n\n**Services Sector:** Growing rapidly (finance, telecommunications, retail, transport).\n* Economic development is a key priority, with efforts to diversify economies, add value to primary products, improve infrastructure, and foster regional trade and integration (e.g., through SADC - Southern African Development Community, EAC - East African Community, COMESA).\n\n(Suggested insertion: A map showing major agricultural cash crop zones (coffee, tea, cotton) and key wildlife tourism areas in East/Southern Africa. Another map showing major mining areas for gold, diamonds, copper, platinum. Images of coffee/tea plantations, a safari scene, and a major industrial area or port in the region.)",
