          "title": "12.3 Climate Change Impacts: Sea‐Level Rise, Extreme Weather, Biodiversity Loss",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/6636354/pexels-photo-6636354.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s98c7EPwLwk",
          "content": "**Global Climate Change:** (Building on Ch 2.4, focus on broader geographical impacts).\nThe long-term alteration of Earth's climate system, primarily due to human activities increasing atmospheric concentrations of greenhouse gases (CO₂, methane, N₂O).\n* **Key GHG Sources:** Burning fossil fuels (energy, transport, industry), deforestation, agriculture.\n\n**Major Geographic Impacts of Climate Change:**\n* **Rising Global Temperatures:** Leads to more frequent and intense heatwaves, affecting human health, agriculture, water resources, ecosystems.\n* **Changes in Precipitation Patterns:**\n* Increased rainfall intensity and frequency in some regions (leading to floods, landslides).\n* Decreased rainfall and prolonged droughts in other regions (leading to water scarcity, crop failure, desertification).\n* More variability and unpredictability of rainfall.\n* **Melting Ice and Snow:**\n* Retreat of glaciers and ice sheets (Greenland, Antarctica).\n* Reduced Arctic sea ice extent.\n* Decreased snowpack in mountain regions (affecting seasonal water supply from meltwater).\n* **Sea-Level Rise:**\n* Caused by:\n* **Thermal expansion** of ocean water (water expands as it warms).\n* **Melting of land-based ice** (glaciers and ice sheets).\n* Impacts:\n* Coastal inundation and erosion.\n* Increased risk of coastal flooding from storm surges.\n* Saltwater intrusion into coastal aquifers and estuaries.\n* Threat to low-lying coastal cities, small island nations, and coastal ecosystems (mangroves, salt marshes).\n* **Ocean Acidification:**\n* Absorption of excess atmospheric CO₂ by oceans leads to formation of carbonic acid, lowering ocean pH.\n* Impacts marine organisms with calcium carbonate shells or skeletons (e.g., corals, shellfish, some plankton), disrupting marine food webs.\n* **Increased Frequency and Intensity of Extreme Weather Events:**\n* More intense heatwaves, droughts, heavy rainfall events, floods.\n* Potential for stronger tropical cyclones (hurricanes, typhoons) due to warmer sea surface temperatures.\n* **Impacts on Biodiversity and Ecosystems:**\n* Shifts in species ranges (poleward and to higher altitudes) as climates change.\n* Changes in phenology (timing of biological events, e.g., flowering, migration).\n* Disruption of ecological interactions.\n* Increased risk of species extinction (especially for species unable to adapt or migrate quickly).\n* Damage to specific ecosystems (e.g., coral bleaching due to warmer seas, forest dieback due to drought/pests).\n* **Impacts on Human Systems:**\n* Threats to food security (crop failures, fisheries decline).\n* Water scarcity.\n* Increased health risks (heat stress, vector-borne diseases, malnutrition).\n* Displacement of people (\"climate refugees\").\n* Damage to infrastructure.\n* Economic losses.\n\nAddressing climate change requires global efforts for **mitigation** (reducing GHG emissions) and **adaptation** (adjusting to unavoidable impacts).\n\n(Suggested insertion: A world map highlighting regions particularly vulnerable to specific climate change impacts (e.g., sea-level rise for small islands/deltas, drought in Sahel/Australia, glacier melt in Himalayas/Andes). Images illustrating coral bleaching, coastal flooding, or impact of drought.)",
