          "title": "15.1 Collecting GPS Coordinates and Building Thematic Maps",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/3862628/pexels-photo-3862628.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2CT12rhTiAo",
          "content": "**Field Data Collection with GPS:**\n\n* Using handheld GPS devices or smartphones with GPS capabilities to record the precise geographic coordinates (latitude, longitude, sometimes altitude) of:\n* **Point features:** e.g., locations of specific trees, litter bins, soil sample points, survey respondent locations, historic landmarks.\n* **Line features:** e.g., by walking a path, river bank, or road and collecting a series of points (creating a track log).\n* **Area features (polygons):** e.g., by walking the perimeter of a land use zone or habitat patch.\n* Data collected can also include attributes (e.g., for a tree point: species, height, condition; for a litter bin: type, condition, fill level).\n\n**Importing GPS Data into GIS Software:**\n\n* GPS data (often in .gpx or .kml format, or as tables of coordinates) is imported into GIS software.\n* The points, lines, or polygons are displayed as new data layers on a base map.\n\n**Creating Thematic Maps from Field Data:**\n\n* Once data is in GIS, it can be used to create various thematic maps.\n* **Point Symbol Maps:** Representing point features collected (e.g., different symbols for different tree species).\n* **Graduated Symbol Maps:** Symbol size varies based on an attribute (e.g., size of dot represents number of pedestrians counted at different locations).\n* **Dot Density Maps:** (If survey data includes quantities at points).\n* **Choropleth Maps:** If data is aggregated to predefined areas (e.g., average noise level per neighborhood, if field data was collected and then averaged for zones).\n* Lines representing routes, polygons representing surveyed areas.\n\n**Base Maps:** Field data layers are often overlaid on existing base maps (e.g., topographic maps, satellite imagery, street maps downloaded or streamed into GIS) for context.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A flowchart showing steps: Plan GPS data collection → Collect data in field → Download/Import data to GIS → Process/Clean_data → Create thematic map layer → Overlay with base map. Screenshots of a GIS interface showing imported GPS points and a simple thematic map created from them (e.g., point symbols for different types of amenities observed).)",
