          "title": "15.2 Analyzing Land Use Patterns and Population Distributions (using GIS)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/30741119/pexels-photo-30741119.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O0nuZTWa6_o",
          "content": "**Field Data for Land Use Mapping:**\n* Conducting land use surveys in a study area (e.g., transects, mapping zones by observation or interview).\n* Classifying land use into categories (e.g., residential, commercial, industrial, recreational, agricultural, vacant).\n* Recording locations of different land use types using GPS or by sketching on a base map and later digitizing in GIS.\n\n**GIS Analysis of Land Use Patterns:**\n* Create a polygon layer representing land use zones.\n* Calculate areas of different land use types.\n* Analyze spatial distribution: Are land uses clustered, dispersed, linear?\n* Proximity analysis: e.g., How much residential land is within 500m of a park? How close is industrial land to residential areas?\n* Overlay with other data: e.g., Overlay land use map with a slope map to see if steep slopes are being developed inappropriately. Overlay with population density to see relationship.\n\n**Field Data for Population Characteristics (e.g., from surveys, observations):**\n* If conducting household surveys, GPS locations of surveyed households can be recorded.\n* Demographic data (age, gender, household size, income level - if collected ethically and appropriately) can be linked to these locations as attributes.\n* Observational data (e.g., pedestrian counts at different times/locations).\n\n**GIS Analysis of Population Distribution:**\n* Create dot maps or graduated symbol maps showing population density or specific demographic characteristics if data points are available.\n* If data is aggregated to enumeration areas (e.g., from census), create choropleth maps of population density, age structure, income levels, etc.\n* Analyze spatial patterns of population (e.g., clustering, disparities).\n* Relate population patterns to other factors (e.g., land use, access to services, environmental conditions) using overlay analysis.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A sample land use map created in GIS. A conceptual GIS analysis like finding areas suitable for a new park by overlaying vacant land, population density, and distance from existing parks. A GIS-generated choropleth map of population density for a small area.)",
