          "title": "15.3 Environmental Impact Assessments Using Remote Data",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/29899950/pexels-photo-29899950.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D6sIvxxe_eg",
          "content": "**Remote Sensing Data for EIA:**\n* Satellite imagery or aerial photos provide baseline environmental information for an area before a proposed project (e.g., new road, mine, urban development). Shows existing land cover, vegetation, water bodies, settlements.\n* Can be used to monitor changes over time during and after project implementation (e.g., deforestation, urban sprawl, changes in water quality if discernible).\n\n**Fieldwork for EIA (Ground Truthing and Local Impact):**\n* **Ground Truthing:** Visiting locations identified from remote sensing imagery to verify land cover classifications, assess vegetation types, identify specific features.\n* **Ecological Surveys:** Using quadrats/transects to assess biodiversity, plant species composition, animal presence in areas potentially affected.\n* **Soil and Water Sampling:** Collecting samples for laboratory analysis to assess baseline quality or impact of pollution.\n* **Social Impact Assessment:** Surveys and interviews with local communities to understand potential impacts of project on their livelihoods, health, culture, access to resources.\n\n**GIS Integration:**\n* Overlaying project plans on existing environmental data layers (e.g., protected areas, slopes, floodplains, sensitive habitats) to identify potential conflicts or high-impact zones.\n* Creating impact maps (e.g., mapping area affected by noise pollution, dust, habitat loss).\n* Modeling potential spread of pollutants or other impacts.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A conceptual diagram showing how GIS could overlay a proposed road project layer with sensitive habitat layer and slope layer to identify high-risk sections. A before-and-after satellite image or map illustrating an environmental change (e.g., expansion of a mine or deforestation around a new settlement) that could be part of an EIA study.)",
